    "</div>\r\n<details><summary>原始讨论（英文，仅供参考）</summary><div class=\"source-discussion i18n-noloc\" lang=\"en\">GiorgioGss Highly Voted 6 months, 2 weeks ago<br>Selected Answer: C<br>A - Too Much Custom Development<br>B - Guardrails Are Not Fairness Evaluation Tools<br>D - Model Evaluation Jobs Are Not Continuous Monitoring<br>https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker/latest/dg/clarify-measure-data-bias.html<br>upvoted 8 times<br>blieskies Most Recent 3 weeks, 2 days ago<br>Selected Answer: C<br>I guessed B but see now why it&#x27;s wrong.<br>Guardrails can&#x27;t score outputs on bias but this is what Sagemaker Clarify specifically does - therefore it&#x27;s C<br>upvoted 1 times<br>SSSam261 month ago<br>Selected Answer: D<br>Why D is Correct ?<br>- Compare two prompt approaches : Bedrock Model Evaluation job natively supports comparing prompt variants<br>- Fairness metrics across demographics : Model evaluation provides bias/fairness assessment built-in<br>- Monitoring : Model invocation logging → CloudWatch<br>- Weekly reports : Bedrock evaluation jobs can be scheduled; CloudWatch dashboards provide reporting<br>https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock/latest/userguide/evaluation.html<br>upvoted 1 times<br>QuinnGT1 month, 1 week ago<br>Selected Answer: D<br>Without a doubt, D is the correct answer. It&#x27;s the least amount of custom development and follows their best practice guides.<br>Also, SageMaker Clarify was announced to be shut down 3 months ago, why would they include it in the exam as a recommended choice?<br>upvoted 1 times<br>Sun019211 month, 3 weeks ago<br>Selected Answer: A<br>C seems like a reasonable choice, however it is not an &quot;least custom development&quot; path<br>upvoted 1 times<br>Chibuzo13 months ago<br>Selected Answer: D<br>D wins because it uses Bedrock&#x27;s native evaluation jobs (prompt comparison), native invocation logging (real-time monitoring), and native<br>CloudWatch alarms (threshold alerting) — all configured features, not built o