    "\r\n\r\n<div class=\"ref\">Questions 143–146 refer to the following passage.</div>\r\n<div class=\"passage\">Old Grain Store Reborn as Riverside Food Hall\r\n\r\nAfter sitting empty for almost two decades, the Calder Street Grain Store in Port Ellis reopened last Saturday as a food hall with twenty-six independent vendors. The brick warehouse, built in 1911 to store wheat (1)____ arrived by barge, now houses stalls selling everything from Ethiopian coffee to Korean street food.\r\n\r\nThe building&#x27;s new owners, Nora Abernathy and Kofi Mensah, spent three years restoring the structure. (2)____ &quot;We wanted visitors to feel the history of the place the moment they walk in,&quot; said Ms. Abernathy.\r\n\r\nOpening day drew more than 5,000 people, far more than the owners had expected. (3)____, several vendors sold out of food by mid-afternoon. The owners plan to (4)____ the hall&#x27;s opening hours on weekends once vendors have hired additional staff.</div>\r\n",
