    "\r\n\r\n<div class=\"ref\">Questions 172–175 refer to the following Article.</div>\r\n<div class=\"passage\">Linmouth to Test On-Demand Minibus Service\r\nBy Ifeoma Nwankwo, Staff Writer\r\n\r\nStarting July 1, residents of Linmouth&#x27;s northern neighborhoods will be able to request a ride on a city-run minibus through a smartphone app or by telephone. The six-month trial, approved by the city council on Tuesday, will replace Bus Route 14, which runs once an hour and carries an average of only nine passengers per trip. — [1] —.\r\n\r\nUnlike a regular bus, the new service will not follow a fixed route. Instead, riders will be picked up within a short walk of their homes and taken to any destination inside the service zone, which includes the North Linmouth train station, Eastmoor Medical Center, and the Castlegate Shopping Arcade. — [2] —. Fares will be the same as on regular city buses, and monthly passes will be accepted.\r\n\r\n&quot;Route 14 was costly to run and inconvenient for many of the people who rely on it,&quot; said Thandiwe Dube, the city&#x27;s director of transportation. — [3] —. &quot;With this approach, we can offer more frequent and flexible service for roughly the same cost.&quot;\r\n\r\nFive minibuses, each seating twelve passengers, will be leased for the trial. — [4] —. If the results are positive, the council may extend the service to the eastern districts next year.</div>\r\n",
