    "\r\n\r\n",
    {
      "slot": "question.59"
    },
    "\r\n\r\n<div class=\"ref\">Questions 161–163 refer to the following review.</div>\r\n<div class=\"passage\"><span class=\"ttl\">Dining Out: Saffron &amp; Salt, 42 Kellerman Avenue</span>When chef Nadia Farouk closed her small lunch counter at the Eastgate Market last year, many of her regular customers feared they had eaten the last of her spiced lamb flatbreads. They need not have worried. Ms. Farouk has reopened in a much larger space on Kellerman Avenue, and the flatbreads are still on the menu, along with an expanded choice of dinner dishes inspired by her travels in Morocco and Turkey.\r\nI visited on a busy Friday evening. Although every table was taken, our server was attentive and our food arrived promptly. The grilled vegetable platter was generous enough for two people, but the slow-cooked chicken with apricots was the highlight of the meal. My only complaint concerns the desserts, which were rather ordinary compared with everything else.\r\nPrices are reasonable, with all main courses priced between $16 and $24. Saffron &amp; Salt is open Tuesday through Sunday from 5:00 P.M. to 10:00 P.M. Reservations are recommended on weekends.</div>\r\n",
    {
      "slot": "question.60"
